dave: Heads-up: repro'd a parser ordering bug in the node sample app when reconnects happen super-fast (~50-200ms). We sometimes get a duplicated final token and intermittent JSON parse errors.

ali: Can you drop a tiny repro and logs? browser also?

dave: Node only so far. Repro is a tight EventSource loop that closes & reconnects with short jitter. Example: 
```
// loop-reconnect.js
const loop = async () => {
  for (let i=0;i<500;i++){
    const es = new EventSource(url);
    await sleep(120);
    es.close();
    await sleep(rand(50,180));
  }
}
```
Logs show: partial chunk P, auth refresh + new conn emits chunk Q, then leftover of P arrives -> parser emits Q then P-tail -> final marker twice.

priya: Sounds like the parser swaps to the new socket without guaranteeing the old socket data has been drained. Did you try the SDK's buffering flag?

dave: Yup. Buffering helps but doesn't eliminate it. The root seems to be that fragments can interleave across sockets during auth refresh.

ali: Short-term mitigation idea: server add opaque per-chunk seq header and client drops any chunk with seq <= lastSeen during reconnect. Cheap and effective behind a flag.

priya: Also consider a tiny grace window (30-60ms) before the new parser starts consuming, controlled by a feature flag to avoid regressing latency.

ci-bot: Created ticket RED-4410 and attached logs.

ops-bot: Infra note: this can be exacerbated by TCP lingering in CLOSE_WAIT; adding note to runbook.

dave: I'll open a small PR with seq-drop fallback and a repro job under devex/sampleapp/repro/reconnect-jitter.js. Assigning @ali and @priya to review. Please try the script locally if you can. :thumbsup:
